16px;height:12px"></span>Mid-dark</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#808080;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Mid</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#b3b3b3;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Mid-bright</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#e6e6e6;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Bright</p></div></div><p class="supporting">NASA/JPL · Arizona State University · THEMIS daytime IR controlled mosaic v2 · NASA Mars Trek · Relative daytime infrared brightness only (qualitative): the mosaic ships no verified radiometric scaling, so no temperature, thermal-inertia, rock-abundance or dust-cover values are asserted - the legend deliberately has no units. Daytime IR mixes albedo and thermophysical properties, so bright/dark patches are not by themselves a compositional or hazard classification. Not a thermal-inertia product: the TES inertia map (3 km/px) is far too coarse for this region and is not used. Display-grade: tiles nearest-neighbour resampled 1:1 from source level L=z-1 by team/jezero/basemap/prepare_trek_basemap.mjs; the ~100 m source is upsampled above z9. Source alpha is preserved. The display shares the region display-transform caveats (planetocentric display latitude on Web Mercator).</p><p class="supporting">Coverage: THEMIS daytime infrared controlled mosaic v2 (Oct 2018, ~100 m/px) over the Jezero exploration area: derived display tiles cover 77.137-77.543 E, 18.352-18.570 N (Web-Mercator XYZ, zooms 5-9; above z9 the renderer upsamples the same source).</p><details><summary>Scientific processing and verification</summary><p class="supporting">Relative daytime infrared brightness reveals qualitative image contrast. It is not calibrated surface temperature or thermal inertia.</p><dl class="metadata"><div><dt>Source verification</dt><dd>UNVERIFIED</dd></div><div><dt>Processing verification</dt><dd>UNVERIFIED</dd></div><div><dt>Independent registration</dt><dd>UNVERIFIED</dd></div>