or firm ground)</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#F98B0B;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Orange</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#BC3754;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Red-pink: middle</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#61136E;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Purple</p></div><div><p><span aria-hidden="true" style="background-color:#1E0C45;width:16px;height:12px"></span>Dark purple: darkest, cools fast at night (likely loose sand or dust)</p></div></div><p class="supporting">NASA Mars Odyssey THEMIS · night-IR global mosaic (2018 release) · via NASA JPL Mars Trek · Relative brightness only: the 8-bit mosaic values are not calibrated temperatures and are not thermal-inertia values. Observation period not stated by Mars Trek (the &#x27;oct2018&#x27; in the layer name refers to the 2018 mosaic release). Night-time brightness mainly reflects thermal inertia (brighter = holds heat, e.g. rock or indurated surfaces; darker = loose sand or dust), but it can also vary with season, local time and dust. Mosaic of many orbits with stretched brightness between images, so strip-to-strip seams can appear. Colours are a display ramp (matplotlib &#x27;inferno&#x27;) applied to the uncalibrated 8-bit values; they do not mark temperature or thermal-inertia thresholds. Display-grade, nearest-neighbour resampled; not terrain-safety evidence.</p><p class="supporting">Coverage: Mars Trek THEMIS night-IR global mosaic over the team-chosen Oxia display area, 17.11-19.15 N, 334.60-336.60 E: 75 derived display tiles, Web-Mercator XYZ, zooms 5-10 (source level 9, about 81 m per pixel), coloured with an inferno display ramp. No gaps in this area.</p><details><summary>Source and processing details</summary><a href="https://trek.nasa.gov/tiles/Mars/EQ/THEMIS_NightIR_ControlledMosaics_100m_v2_oct2018/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA JPL Mars Trek layer &#x27;MO THEMIS-IR Night, Global Mosaic&#x27; (THEMIS_NightIR_ControlledMosaics_100m_v2_oct2018)</a><p class="supporting">Mars Trek is listed in the Space Apps 2026 challenge resources.</p><p class="supporting">Mars Trek description: geodetically controlled THEMIS night-IR mosaic tied to USGS Viking MDIM 2.1, orthoprojected at 100 m/pixel; 2017 preliminary release with further adjustments in the 2018 release.</p><p class="supporting">WMTS (checked 2026-11-14): EPSG::104905 (Mars 2000 sphere), TopLeftCorner -180/90, 256 px gray+alpha PNG tiles, levels 0-9 (level 9 is the finest; level 10 returns 404).</p><p class="supporting">Display tiles /maps/oxia/themis-night-tiles/ built by team/oxia/thermal/prepare_themis_night.mjs (nearest-neighbour, source gray values unchanged and coloured for display with the matplotlib inferno ramp, source alpha kept); see team/oxia/thermal/build_report.json.</p><p class="supporting">Pixel values are relative 8-bit brightness, not calibrated temperature (Mars Trek states no units or calibration).</p><p class="supporting">Observation period not confirmed: Mars Trek FGDC metadata has placeholder dates (19700101) and status &#x27;In work&#x27;.</p></details></div></section><section aria-label="Basemaps"><h3>Basemaps</h3><p class="supporting">Optical context and mission imagery.</p><p class="supporting">No dataset available.</p></section><section aria-label="Map overlays"><h3>Map overlays</h3><p class="supporting">Recorded traverses and dataset coverage outlines; these do not establish waypoint eligibility.</p><p class="supporting">No dataset available.</p></section><h3>Interface preview</h3><p class="supporting">Demonstration controls below affect schematic artwork only.</p><label class="check-row"><input type="checkbox"/>Show schematic preview <span class="badge science">Scenario</span></label><button class="full">View scientific data register</button>'
